      "role": "user",
      "content": "that actually makes a lot of sense. we've basically been sitting around waiting for luck to strike, but controlling the thermal environment might actually work. \n\nit's just so strange how these problems feel less like math or physics and more like something... i don't know, less logical. it's like the component is actively resisting our attempts to fix it. when you're dealing with gear that's this \"skittish\" or \"mischievous,\" do you usually just treat it as a standard hardware failure, or is there a certain level of—i guess you'd say—\"attitude\" that engineers acknowledge when a specific piece of kit just refuses to cooperate?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
